    "justification": "The proposition supplies every learned complete-normal rate used by the attainment proof.",
    "gap": "It does not imply nonparametric \\(L_4(P_X)\\) rates for continuously distributed covariates from fourth moments alone.",
    "consumer": "It fixes the supported first-stage and threshold regime for the optional backend.",
    "proof_tex": "There are finitely many support points, cells, periods, normal coordinates, target coordinates, and folds.  The lower support-mass and overlap conditions make every training count used by a conditional cell average of order \\(n\\) in probability.  Every coordinate of \\(Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}\\) is a finite sum of outcome residuals multiplied by inverse propensities bounded by \\(\\varepsilon^{-1}\\); the same is true of \\(\\psi_P^0\\), apart from bounded conditional means and the target-share correction.  Fixed \\(T\\), the conditional fourth-moment envelope, and Jensen's inequality imply, at this fixed law,\n\\[\n\\sup_{x:P_X(x)>0}E_P[\\|Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}\\|^4+|\\psi_P^0|^4\\mid X=x]<\\infty.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nThus every coordinate of \\(Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}\\prime}\\) and \\(Z_P^{\\mathrm{per}}\\psi_P^0\\) has finite conditional variance.  Chebyshev's inequality and a union bound give root-\\(n\\) accuracy for all oracle training-cell averages.\n\nThe cell-average estimates of \\(p\\), \\(m\\), \\(\\pi\\), and the preliminary ratios are jointly \\(O_P(n^{-1/2})\\).  Identity (1) in Lemma \\(\\ref{lem:orthogonal-normal-signal}\\) shows, in particular, that replacing \\(Z\\) by the orthogonal signal \\(\\widehat S\\) creates no first-order regression-centering drift: its conditional mean error is \\(O_P(n^{-1})\\).  The finite-dimensional mean-value inequality and H\\\"older's inequality applied to the second moments and cross-moments then give\n\\[\n\\max_\\ell\\|\\widehat K^{-\\ell}-K_P\\|_{P_X,4,\\mathrm{op}}\n+\\max_\\ell\\|\\widehat r^{-\\ell}-r_P\\|_{P_X,4}\n=O_P(n^{-1/2}).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAt a support point \\(x\\), let \\(\\kappa_P(x)\\) be the smallest positive eigenvalue of \\(K_P(x)\\), with value \\(+\\infty\\) at rank zero.  Finiteness of the support implies\n\\[\n\\underline\\kappa_P=\\min_{x:P_X(x)>0}\\kappa_P(x)>0.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nThis is a pointwise consequence, not a uniform model restriction.  The eigenvalue variational inequality used in (7) and (12) show that, once \\(\\tau_n<\\underline\\kappa_P/2\\), a rank error requires \\(\\|\\widehat K^{-\\ell}(x)-K_P(x)\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}>\\tau_n\\).  Fourth-power Markov inequality and \\(n^{-1/2}/\\tau_n\\to0\\) therefore prove rank recovery uniformly over the finite support and folds.\n\nOn the recovery event, all retained eigenvalues exceed \\(\\underline\\kappa_P/2\\).  Repeating identity (9) with \\((K_P,r_P)\\) in place of \\((H,g)\\) yields\n\\[\n\\|\\{\\widehat K^{-\\ell}_\\tau\\}^{\\dagger}\\widehat r^{-\\ell}-K_P^\\dagger r_P\\|_{P_X,4}\n=O_P(n^{-1/2}).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nUsing only \\(\\|\\{\\widehat K^{-\\ell}_\\tau\\}^\\dagger\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le\\tau_n^{-1}\\) gives the unconditional conservative bound \\(O_P(n^{-1/2}/\\tau_n)\\).  With \\(\\tau_n=n^{-a}\\), this is \\(O_P(n^{-1/2+a})\\), and\n\\[\nn^{-1+2a}=o(n^{-1/2})\\quad(0<a<1/4),\n\\qquad\nn^{-1/2+a}n^{-1/2}=o(n^{-1/2})\\quad(0<a<1/2).\n\\]\nThe declared range \\(0<a<1/4\\) proves all remaining assertions.",
